  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nCurrently through Sunday...\n\nBeen a quiet, pleasant start to the weekend across the\nregion...with dry conditions continuing the rest of today into\ntonight. The pattern across the CONUS is a well-amplified one. \nUpper air and satellite data showing sharper north-northwesterly\nflow in place across the Central Plains...sandwiched between \nhigh pressure centered over the Desert SW with northward-\nextending ridging and an area of low pressure making its way \nacross the Midwest. Satellite imagery also showing that outside \nof a bit of scattered lower-level cumulus as well as some \nhigher-level thin smoke from wildfires off to the north-\nnorthwest, continues to be plenty of sunshine out there. At the \nsurface, high pressure remains the primary feature, gradually \nshifting east with time. This is keeping winds north- \nnortheasterly this afternoon, some gusts near 20 MPH, especially\nacross the eastern half of the forecast area. Not looking at \nany surprises as far as temps go, topping out in the mid-80s for\nmuch of the area. \n\nExpecting quiet conditions to continue on into this evening and \ntonight...winds becoming light/variable and eventually more \nsoutherly as that surface ridging continues pushing east. Not \nout of the question that some patchy fog could develop around \ndawn...mainly across eastern portions of the area where the \nlightest winds (potentially outright calm) are expected to be. \nNot expecting widespread coverage, looking like mainly in lower-\nlying areas. \n\nFor Sunday, no notable changes made to the forecast, which\nremains dry. Models showing continued north-northwesterly flow\naloft for the area...but off to our west, a larger-scale \ntrough/low pressure system is working its way into the Pac \nNW/West Coast, starting to dampen the ridging. As the sfc high \npressure system continues pushing east, troughing is deepening \nover the High Plains, spreading the south-southeaster winds \narea-wide...far western areas could have some gusts near 20 MPH \nthrough the afternoon hours. Temperatures are forecast to be a \ntouch warmer...with mid-80s in the east to low-90s further west.\n\nNew work week...\n\nThe main story for the upcoming new work week is the transition\nfrom an amplified upper level pattern to one that more zonal \nin nature. \n\nMonday is the day where the brunt of this transition occurs...as \nmodels show the larger upper level low pressure system shifting \nfurther into central Canada, and the accompanying troughing \nextending south well into the Rockies. An approaching surface \ncold front will be working its way south through the day...but \nmodels are in good agreement keeping it NW of the forecast area \nthrough the afternoon hours. This will bring the potential for \nbreezy south- southeasterly winds, resulting from a tighter \npressure gradient...sustained speeds around 15-20 MPH will be \npossible. Also expecting another bump up in temperatures, with \nforecast highs around 90 in the east to around 100 in the \nsouthwest. \n\nMonday evening, this sfc frontal boundary will be the focus for \nthunderstorm development...but there is some uncertainty with \nthe overall coverage given the better upper level forcing is \nbetter focused to the north. Into the overnight hours, models \nshow a strengthening low-level jet, with the better convergence \nalong its edge remaining focused along our north-northwestern \nportions of the forecast area. Because of those lingering \ncoverage uncertainties, precipitation chances remain on the \nlower side, mainly in the 20-30 percent range. \n\nThe forecast through the rest of the work week is driven by the \npotential for periodic upper level shortwave disturbances to \npass through the region in that zonal/progressive flow. Models \nare in decent agreement with the timing...with the better \noverall chances favoring the evening-overnight hours. More \nuncertainty lies with the location and coverage...as that will \nbe dependent on the location of any surface/low-level \nboundaries...which could be greatly impacted by any activity \nfrom the previous day. Hard to have ton of confidence in the \nfiner details the further out in time one goes. As far as \ntemperatures go...forecast highs are cooler mid-week, with more \n70s-80s for Tue-Wed...warming back up into the 80s-90s by the \nend of the week.",
